import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.AuRYXM_l.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy game design specification","description":"Version 0.2, October 8, 2026. English is confirmed for the first version. Required concepts come from the baseline; quantities, narrative, scoring and content below are propos","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy game design specification","description":"Version 0.2, October 8, 2026. English is confirmed for the first version. Required concepts come from the baseline; quantities, narrative, scoring and content below are propos","source":"docs/game-design.md","category":"Game & system design","sourceUpdated":"2026-10-08T06:33:54+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/game-design.md","filePath":"content/docs/game-design.md"}'),r={name:"content/docs/game-design.md"};function s(o,e,c,d,l,p){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div><h1 id="chainy-game-design-specification">Chainy game design specification<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-game-design-specification" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>Version 0.2, October 8, 2026. English is confirmed for the first version. Required concepts come from the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/project-baseline.html">baseline</a>; quantities, narrative, scoring and content below are proposed implementation details for team review. No prototype or playtest success is claimed.</p><h2 id="product-and-boundaries">Product and boundaries<a class="header-anchor" href="#product-and-boundaries" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Chainy is an offline, single-player Android game for ages 13–17 at Cryptita Plays outreach and mini-library sites. Two compact themed areas host three educational levels. The learner explores, collects context, constructs/configures a system, tests it, explains its behavior, and completes a short assessment. Construction permits different valid arrangements within bounded space; it is not a single prescribed drag-and-drop solution.</p><p>The pre-final must demonstrate core modules, completed manuscript chapters and pilot evidence by November 21, 2026. Use one reusable exploration scene with themed areas and activity overlays rather than two large open worlds. Proposed cap: 12 placeable objects per workbench, three local ledger nodes, two classifier classes initially, and one agent route. These limits may change after review; the four required mechanics cannot be silently removed.</p><p>Real cryptocurrency, wallets, public blockchain connections, multiplayer, cloud AI, voice recognition, automatic cloud sync and a teacher website are outside the recommended pre-final implementation. The teacher workflow lives in a small staff-facing export screen and external aggregation tool.</p><h2 id="player-loop-and-navigation">Player loop and navigation<a class="header-anchor" href="#player-loop-and-navigation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Boot → learner code/profile → main menu → map → mission briefing → exploration → workbench/activity → feedback/retry → quiz → badge/progress save → map.</p><p>Continue resumes the last valid checkpoint. Exiting an unfinished activity preserves the last committed checkpoint and marks its session interrupted; it never invents a passing attempt. Pause exposes audio, text options, return-to-map and switch-learner confirmation. Switching learner ends the current session before a new profile becomes active.</p><p>Exploration uses a virtual stick and context interaction button. Construction mode separates placement from movement: choose a part, preview a valid location, place, select/configure, connect ports, test, undo last edit, or reset after confirmation. A blocked placement explains why. Touch buttons offer alternatives to precise dragging. Camera is a fixed top-down/isometric follow view; no free camera system is needed.</p><h2 id="learning-objectives-and-levels">Learning objectives and levels<a class="header-anchor" href="#learning-objectives-and-levels" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Learning objective</th><th>Player action</th><th>Feedback and success</th><th>Assessment evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Records and links</td><td>Explain records, ordering rules and links</td><td>Collect delivery records, configure data in blocks, connect them in a valid sequence</td><td>Show order violations and missing predecessor links; accept multiple spatial layouts with the same valid logical order</td><td>Explain why a dependent event follows its prerequisite and what a predecessor reference represents</td></tr><tr><td>2 Verify and repair</td><td>Detect altered records and explain replica agreement under a simplified rule</td><td>Compare hashes/reference copies, edit a bad record, inspect three local replicas and choose reconciliation</td><td>Distinguish changed data, broken link and disagreement; repair against the exercise&#39;s trusted reference</td><td>Identify evidence of change and explain why a hash alone does not prove truth</td></tr><tr><td>3 Examples and decisions</td><td>Train a tiny classifier, evaluate mistakes and distinguish an agent&#39;s action policy</td><td>Label examples, train, predict held-out objects, inspect errors, revise examples, configure a delivery agent</td><td>Show prediction and feature-based explanation; route succeeds/fails under visible rules</td><td>Identify a bad example, interpret a wrong prediction, and explain observe–decide–act</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Proposed session budget is 30–45 minutes across all levels, with short breaks and 8–12 minutes of activity per level; validate this with learners. Avoid promising completion time without pilot evidence.</p><h3 id="level-1">Level 1<a class="header-anchor" href="#level-1" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Narrative proposal: restore a community learning center&#39;s delivery records. Give learners three records with explicit causal rules, such as pack before dispatch before receive. Spatial arrangement is free; validation depends on data/link rules, not matching a hidden picture. Start with one scaffolded example, then remove one hint. A block includes an ID, payload, previous-reference and computed digest. Display shortened digests but use full canonical values for checks.</p><p>Completion requires a valid sequence, a successful verification action and the level quiz. Wrong construction is retryable without loss of all exploration progress. A hint explains a concept instead of simply revealing the answer.</p><h3 id="level-2">Level 2<a class="header-anchor" href="#level-2" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Use three logical copies of a small ledger in the same app. One exercise changes a record without recomputing the following link. Another shows that recomputing local hashes does not establish historical correctness: compare against the scenario&#39;s trusted checkpoint/copies.</p><p>The toy agreement rule is explicit: in this exercise at most one of three replicas is corrupted and two matching valid copies select the repair candidate. If all copies differ, report no agreement and request the trusted checkpoint; do not pick arbitrarily. Label this a simplified classroom rule, not a secure general consensus algorithm. No device-to-device network or mining is involved.</p><p>Allow the learner to arrange and connect replica objects on the workbench, inspect their records, introduce a test change and observe effects. Reconciliation is an explicit action with a preview. Do not teach that changing one block makes all stored blocks disappear.</p><h3 id="level-3">Level 3<a class="header-anchor" href="#level-3" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Use fictional parcel types with visible normalized numeric features, such as weight and fragility, rather than camera images or real personal data. Learners assign labels to a small set, then a C# nearest-centroid model computes a mean feature vector for each class. The nearest prototype supplies a prediction; a deterministic tie is presented as ambiguous rather than fabricated certainty. Explain the features and do not call distance a calibrated confidence probability.</p><p>Keep held-out test items separate from training items. Include an intentionally misleading example set so learners see that more or poor-quality examples do not guarantee better predictions. Retraining affects predictions; changing a UI label without changing the model is not sufficient.</p><p>The agent activity is a separate visible rule-based system: observe an obstacle/parcel condition, choose an action policy, execute stepwise, inspect the trace. Proposed action palette: move, turn, pick up, deliver, stop. A small capped sequence with one condition is sufficient; avoid creating a general programming language or claiming reinforcement learning.</p><h2 id="assessment-and-progression">Assessment and progression<a class="header-anchor" href="#assessment-and-progression" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Proposed formative quiz: three objective-aligned questions per level with explanations and retries. Provisional unlock: complete the activity and answer at least two of three correctly. Preserve first-attempt results separately from eventual completion, including hints/retries. Quizzes are learning feedback, not a validated research instrument; research pre/post forms are separately versioned.</p><p>Examples for educator review:</p><ul><li>L1: A received event appears before dispatch. Which stated rule fails, and how would you correct it?</li><li>L2: A player changes data and recomputes local hashes. What additional reference would you need to detect the historical change?</li><li>L3: The model misclassifies a test parcel after poor labels. What could you inspect/change, and why might adding more poor labels fail?</li></ul><p>Badges represent completed activities, not certified mastery. No currency, purchases, leaderboards or certificate system is needed. Record total completion without comparing learners publicly.</p><h2 id="screens-and-presentation">Screens and presentation<a class="header-anchor" href="#screens-and-presentation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Screen</th><th>Essential elements</th><th>Failure or accessibility behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Learner entry</td><td>Study code, learner code, continue, help</td><td>Typo explanation; no claim of verified login or automatic cloud restore</td></tr><tr><td>Map</td><td>Three levels, two area themes, progress</td><td>Explain locked levels; continue from saved checkpoint</td></tr><tr><td>Mission/HUD</td><td>Objective, interaction prompt, pause</td><td>Brief English instructions; no mandatory audio-only instruction</td></tr><tr><td>Workbench</td><td>Palette, placement preview, configuration, links, test, reset</td><td>Selection alternatives to dragging; text/icon feedback alongside color</td></tr><tr><td>Classifier</td><td>Labeled examples, train, held-out test, error explanation</td><td>Missing class and tie states are visible</td></tr><tr><td>Agent</td><td>Environment, rule sequence, step/run/reset, trace</td><td>Stop looping/invalid moves after a bounded step count</td></tr><tr><td>Quiz</td><td>Prompt, response, explanation, next</td><td>Prevent accidental double submission; keep first attempt</td></tr><tr><td>Teacher export</td><td>Study/session scope, record count, export, receipt status</td><td>Cancellation is safe; no delete-on-export</td></tr><tr><td>Settings</td><td>Audio, text/readability controls</td><td>Retain readable contrast and avoid flashing reward effects</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use a readable low-poly style suitable for teens: practical data/technology scenarios, restrained rewards and short explanations. Reuse one avatar, one guide rig with different presentation, a small environment kit and a shared block/node kit. Storyboards are flow references; their lesson counts and visual polish are not delivery commitments.</p><h2 id="asset-and-content-contracts">Asset and content contracts<a class="header-anchor" href="#asset-and-content-contracts" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Luis owns source models/materials and license inventory; Miguel owns UI prefabs/layout; Christian owns scene integration; Sherwin and Sean review instructional accuracy. Use clear names such as CH_Block_Record and CH_UI_QuizPanel. Store editable sources separately from exported game assets in the game repo as appropriate; commit Unity metadata, avoid editing the same scene concurrently, and preserve third-party license text.</p><p>Every activity definition has an ID, version, level, objective, instructions, required components, validation rules, hint text and quiz references. Frozen study builds use immutable content versions; changes produce a new version. Local content includes no remote URLs required for play.</p><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/technical-design.html">architecture</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/hardware-validation.html">hardware targets</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/qa-evaluation.html">QA protocol</a> and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/team-delegation.html">team packets</a> define the implementation and review handoffs.</p><h2 id="traceability-and-production-handoff">Traceability and production handoff<a class="header-anchor" href="#traceability-and-production-handoff" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/requirements/product-requirements.html">product requirements</a> define acceptance and decision status. This GDD is the gameplay contract, not a second backlog. Use existing CH issues for execution; do not create duplicate mechanic tasks solely because a new document names them.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Gameplay contract</th><th>Requirements</th><th>Implementing tasks</th><th>Independent review focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Exploration and configurable construction</td><td>CH-R01, CH-R02</td><td>CH-05, CH-26</td><td>Touch usability; two different valid layouts; no hidden spatial answer</td></tr><tr><td>Level 1 records/order</td><td>CH-R03, CH-R08</td><td>CH-06</td><td>Explicit causal rules, link meaning and first-attempt evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Level 2 integrity/replicas</td><td>CH-R04, CH-R05</td><td>CH-07</td><td>Trusted comparison, divergent replicas and no-agreement behavior</td></tr><tr><td>Level 3 classifier/agent</td><td>CH-R06, CH-R07, CH-R08</td><td>CH-08, CH-09</td><td>Examples affect model; held-out test; policy and trace agree</td></tr><tr><td>Profile, checkpoint, badge and replay</td><td>CH-R08, CH-R09</td><td>CH-04, CH-06, CH-26</td><td>Commit before success; profile isolation and historical attempts</td></tr><tr><td>Staff collection and recovery</td><td>CH-R10, CH-R11</td><td>CH-04, CH-11, CH-14</td><td>Retry/cancel safety; export is distinct from receipt and transfer</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="activity-authoring-contract">Activity authoring contract<a class="header-anchor" href="#activity-authoring-contract" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Recommended content IDs follow <code>CH-L1-ORDER-01</code>, <code>CH-L2-TAMPER-01</code>, <code>CH-L2-REPLICAS-01</code>, <code>CH-L3-CLASSIFY-01</code> and <code>CH-L3-AGENT-01</code>. These are proposed activity identifiers, not new levels or additional mandatory scope. Freeze actual IDs during CH-02 so rules, UI, stored attempts and assessment references agree. Do not rename an ID in a frozen study build without an explicit content-version decision.</p><p>Each activity handoff must contain:</p><ol><li>An ID/version, level, linked CH-R IDs and one observable learning objective.</li><li>Initial objects/data, the learner&#39;s available actions and any quantity/step limits.</li><li>A deterministic validation rule and named result categories with learner-facing feedback.</li><li>A success example and at least one meaningful failure/counterexample, using synthetic data.</li><li>Retry/reset behavior, a safe resume checkpoint and the data needed to restore it.</li><li>Hint text, an assessment reference and a short explanation prompt that tests reasoning.</li><li>Editable source owner, consuming scene/prefab or data asset, and a reviewer check.</li></ol><p>Rules agents can develop against synthetic activity definitions while UI/art agents work from the same IDs and result categories. They must not independently invent schema fields or mutate each other&#39;s scenes. Unity services return semantic results; presentation chooses text/icon treatment. The integration owner connects them in one owned scene after contracts agree.</p><h3 id="minimum-exercise-and-failure-contract">Minimum exercise and failure contract<a class="header-anchor" href="#minimum-exercise-and-failure-contract" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><table><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Learner reasoning to make visible</th><th>Proposed result categories and recovery</th><th>Content review check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordering</td><td>Predict which event requires another event first, then test</td><td>Valid; missing record; wrong order; missing/broken predecessor. Highlight the violated rule; allow edit and retest</td><td>Moving correct blocks on screen does not change logical validity</td></tr><tr><td>Tamper detection</td><td>Explain which reference establishes a mismatch</td><td>Unchanged; data mismatch; broken link; missing trusted reference. Do not declare historical correctness when the reference is unavailable</td><td>Alter-and-rehash still differs from the supplied trusted checkpoint</td></tr><tr><td>Replica agreement</td><td>Predict the effect of one disagreeing copy</td><td>Agreement; repair candidate; no agreement. Preview explicit repair; all-different requires the exercise reference</td><td>Two matching valid copies are accepted only under the stated exercise assumptions</td></tr><tr><td>Classification</td><td>Predict how the chosen examples affect an unseen parcel</td><td>Not trained; insufficient classes/examples; predicted label; ambiguous tie. Explain feature distances, revise labels/examples and retrain</td><td>At least one designed held-out prediction changes after training-data revision; labels alone are not hard-coded predictions</td></tr><tr><td>Agent delivery</td><td>Explain the next action from observation and policy</td><td>Running; delivered; invalid action; blocked; step limit reached. Stop safely, preserve trace, edit policy and reset scenario</td><td>Identical scenario/policy gives the same trace; success cannot be a UI-only flag</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Exact result identifiers are a technical interface decision; the meanings above are the GDD contract. A UI agent may refine wording without changing success conditions. Tie handling must be deterministic and show ambiguity rather than silently selecting an unexplained class. Assessment questions must not reveal the target label of a held-out item before prediction if that item is being used to evaluate understanding.</p><h2 id="progression-feedback-and-interruption-rules">Progression, feedback and interruption rules<a class="header-anchor" href="#progression-feedback-and-interruption-rules" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The proposed progression order is Level 1 → Level 2 → Level 3; reviewing a completed level is always available. Keep prerequisites and the proposed two-of-three quiz threshold in versioned content so a reviewed change does not require scattered hard-coded checks. Until CH-02 accepts that threshold it remains a recommendation, not a school pass mark.</p><p>Activity success and quiz success are different facts. A completed construction alone does not satisfy a quiz gate. Failed answers display an explanation and permit retry; the first result remains in evidence. Award a badge once per learner/level completion, retain later attempts separately, and do not multiply badge awards on replay or duplicate submission. Never show a completion badge if saving fails.</p><p>Do not punish concept exploration by deleting previously committed progress. A reset restores the current activity&#39;s initial scenario after confirmation; it does not erase profile history. An interrupted workbench resumes the last committed checkpoint, which may omit edits made since that checkpoint. UI copy must explain that boundary. Switch-profile, return-to-map and reset confirmations must distinguish discarding uncommitted edits from deleting learner evidence.</p><p>Feedback sequence: show the learner&#39;s outcome → identify the relevant rule or observation → suggest a next inspection/action → let the learner revise. Use a hint escalation from concept reminder to worked example; record assistance according to the approved evidence contract. Rewards stay brief and never obscure the explanation.</p><h2 id="learning-and-simulation-review-gate">Learning and simulation review gate<a class="header-anchor" href="#learning-and-simulation-review-gate" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Before an activity enters the integrated study build, Sherwin checks the scenario and required scope, Sean reviews objective/question alignment, Christian reviews deterministic rules, and Miguel/Luis check readable presentation. This is a proposed review division within the named team roles, not evidence that reviews occurred.</p><ul><li><strong>Learning-design verdict: validate first.</strong> The loop supports prediction, testing and explanation, but completion and enjoyment do not demonstrate learning gain. Observable next check: an educator-reviewed objective-to-item mapping and an internal walkthrough exposing at least one misconception per concept group.</li><li><strong>Simulation-accuracy verdict: proceed with stated simplifications.</strong> A digest comparison can reveal a mismatch with the exercise reference; it cannot certify truth. Three on-device replicas do not demonstrate real distributed security. The classifier learns class prototypes from examples; the delivery agent follows a visible policy. Observable next check: run the altered-and-rehashed ledger, all-different replicas, poor labels, tie and failing-policy examples and inspect both outcomes and explanatory text.</li><li><strong>Scope verdict: validate first.</strong> Prototype one bounded exercise per required mechanic before multiplying environments/content. Reducing props, narrative length or question variants preserves required concepts; deleting free construction, multi-node behavior, trained classification or the agent does not. Any scope conflict returns to the product owner with the affected requirement and evidence gap.</li></ul><p>No learner approvals, actual playtest results, final device support or implementation completion are established by this GDD. Site-specific language/access needs and the real pilot arrangements remain explicit dependencies in the PRD and QA protocol.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
